1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,957
Language models now work in teams.

2
00:00:03,307 --> 00:00:06,709
How should a team of hundreds, or a thousand, be organized?

3
00:00:08,133 --> 00:00:14,380
Most multi-agent systems rely on a coordinator:
one agent that reads every report and gives every order.

4
00:00:14,730 --> 00:00:18,375
Honeybee swarms, ant colonies
and bird flocks work differently.

5
00:00:18,725 --> 00:00:22,289
They coordinate through local interactions,
with no one directing the whole.

6
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:26,858
A honeybee swarm chooses its new home without a leader.

7
00:00:27,208 --> 00:00:31,457
Scouts inspect cavities themselves
and dance longer for better ones.

8
00:00:31,807 --> 00:00:35,104
When enough scouts gather at one cavity, the swarm commits.

9
00:00:35,454 --> 00:00:39,611
We borrow first-hand reports,
graded advertising and commitment by quorum.

10
00:00:41,033 --> 00:00:43,727
Ants mark the paths they walk, and the marks fade.

11
00:00:44,077 --> 00:00:47,920
A shorter path is renewed faster,
so the colony converges on it.

12
00:00:48,270 --> 00:00:51,474
We borrow shared marks that lose
their hold unless they are renewed.

13
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:55,988
In a flock, each bird follows only its nearest neighbours.

14
00:00:56,338 --> 00:01:00,855
A few birds that know the way can guide the rest,
and none flies alone past the hawks.

15
00:01:01,205 --> 00:01:04,293
We borrow relay between neighbours, with no central store.

16
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:12,167
We tested a coordinator against designs built on these mechanisms,
on the same tasks, in three test beds.

17
00:01:12,517 --> 00:01:19,158
Teams grew to 1,024 language-model agents,
hubs failed, and model calls took time.

18
00:01:20,567 --> 00:01:27,765
Here are two recorded episodes of a wildfire rescue
with 128 drones, run by the same language model.

19
00:01:28,115 --> 00:01:30,948
On the left, a coordinator assigns every drone.

20
00:01:31,298 --> 00:01:35,860
On the right, NatureSwarm: each drone
decides from what nearby drones relay.

21
00:01:36,210 --> 00:01:42,277
The coordinator's team rescued 69% of the victims.
NatureSwarm rescued 93%.

22
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:46,126
The paper's main result shows the same pattern.

23
00:01:46,476 --> 00:01:53,315
With 128 drones, coordinators run by smaller
language models saved 41% of victims.

24
00:01:53,665 --> 00:01:57,949
Drones using insect-inspired mechanisms saved 79%.

25
00:01:59,367 --> 00:02:04,847
The decline came from assigning work across the whole team,
far more than from reading longer prompts.

26
00:02:05,197 --> 00:02:12,290
A simple law fitted on small teams predicted it:
15 of 17 results in larger teams fell inside its range.

27
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:22,082
With 1,024 drones, NatureSwarm run by a small model came close
to a frontier model's coordinator at under a third of the price.

28
00:02:22,432 --> 00:02:26,937
Once model calls took time, NatureSwarm
outperformed even that coordinator.

29
00:02:27,287 --> 00:02:30,956
It also kept teams working when radio links or the base failed.

30
00:02:32,367 --> 00:02:35,211
Every animation in this video is a recorded episode.

31
00:02:35,561 --> 00:02:43,735
The release includes the three test beds, every design,
the experiment scripts, and this viewer, which plays any episode step by step.

32
00:02:45,167 --> 00:02:49,311
Mechanisms borrowed from nature help
where central control reaches its limits.

33
00:02:49,661 --> 00:02:52,901
And a law fitted on small teams indicates where those limits lie.
